WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.037
This is Allen Company training on fighting small

00:07.037 --> 00:10.338
fires. Fire needs three things: heat,

00:10.338 --> 00:13.888
fuel and oxygen. Take one away and it goes out.

00:13.888 --> 00:16.663
Water takes away heat. Shutting off fuel takes

00:16.663 --> 00:19.625
away fuel. Smothering takes away oxygen.

00:19.625 --> 00:22.488
Dry chemical extinguishers also break the chemical

00:22.488 --> 00:23.912
reaction itself.

00:25.082 --> 00:27.832
Fires are grouped by class. Class A:

00:27.832 --> 00:30.619
ordinary combustibles like wood, paper,

00:30.619 --> 00:33.419
rubber and conveyor belting. Class B:

00:33.419 --> 00:36.769
flammable liquids like diesel, oil and grease.

00:36.769 --> 00:39.869
Class C: energized electrical equipment.

00:39.869 --> 00:43.044
Our sites and equipment carry ABC dry chemical

00:43.044 --> 00:46.032
extinguishers, from 5 to 20 pounds,

00:46.032 --> 00:49.807
good for all three, and some carbon dioxide extinguishers,

00:49.807 --> 00:51.832
for classes B and C.

00:55.150 --> 00:57.663
Only fight a fire if you've sounded the alarm,

00:57.663 --> 01:00.175
the fire is small and not spreading,

01:00.175 --> 01:02.325
you have the right extinguisher, and your way out

01:02.325 --> 01:04.975
is behind you. If any of those isn't true,

01:04.975 --> 01:07.562
get out and warn others. Underground,

01:07.562 --> 01:10.638
don't let a fire get between you and your escapeway.

01:13.952 --> 01:17.527
Use PASS. Pull the pin, breaking the tamper seal.

01:17.527 --> 01:20.990
Aim low, at the base of the fire, not the flames.

01:20.990 --> 01:23.752
Squeeze the handle. Sweep side to side across the

01:23.752 --> 01:27.027
base until it's out. Start six to ten feet back

01:27.027 --> 01:30.215
and move in as it goes down. A typical extinguisher

01:30.215 --> 01:33.590
empties in 10 to 20 seconds, so don't waste it.

01:34.706 --> 01:37.006
Back away; don't turn your back on it,

01:37.006 --> 01:40.381
because it can flare up again. Report every use;

01:40.381 --> 01:43.794
a used extinguisher must be recharged or replaced,

01:43.794 --> 01:46.156
even if it was only used for a second.

01:47.276 --> 01:49.776
If your machine catches fire: stop,

01:49.776 --> 01:52.864
shut it down, set the brake, trigger the fire suppression

01:52.864 --> 01:56.288
system if it has one, and get off. Only then decide

01:56.288 --> 01:57.464
if you can fight it.

02:00.774 --> 02:03.374
Extinguishers are inspected visually at least once

02:03.374 --> 02:05.999
a month, and get a maintenance check at least every

02:05.999 --> 02:08.974
twelve months. On your pre-shift, make sure yours

02:08.974 --> 02:11.249
is there, the gauge is in the green,

02:11.249 --> 02:14.237
and the pin and seal are intact. The safety manager

02:14.237 --> 02:16.599
will go over extinguisher use with you the same

02:16.599 --> 02:18.299
day as this course.

02:19.416 --> 02:21.516
Learn where the extinguishers are before you need

02:21.516 --> 02:24.741
one. At the Level 2 diesel tank at Boonesboro,

02:24.741 --> 02:27.191
for example, there's an extinguisher on a stand

02:27.191 --> 02:30.641
at each end of the tank, and the sign says no smoking,

02:30.641 --> 02:33.353
no open flames. Your site tour will show you the

02:33.353 --> 02:34.216
rest.

02:35.346 --> 02:38.971
Recap: alarm first. Fight only small fires with

02:38.971 --> 02:42.021
your exit behind you. PASS. Back away,

02:42.021 --> 02:45.571
report the use. Check your extinguisher every shift.

02:46.692 --> 02:49.855
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

02:49.855 --> 02:51.355
override this video.
